Description: glDrawBuffer(GL_BACK) of a stereo window reaches both eyes with a shader's own output
 OpenGL 4.6 core, 17.4.1 "Selecting Buffers for Writing": for the default
 framebuffer one glDrawBuffer constant specifies "zero, one, two, or four
 buffers", and they are the set "to which fragment color zero is written".
 GL_BACK of a quad buffer window is the left and the right back buffer.
 .
 _mesa_drawbuffers() lists those buffers as color buffers 0 and 1, and the
 drivers write gl_FragColor to every color buffer, but a shader's own output
 at location 0 only to the first. So a program that draws each eye into
 GL_BACK_LEFT and GL_BACK_RIGHT and then its 2D parts with GL_BACK got those
 parts in the left eye alone when its shaders declare their output (every
 core profile shader does): Open Cascade's CAD Assistant 1.6.0, a Qt Quick
 program on a 4.5 core context, showed its tool strip, its panels and its logo
 in the left eye only, on radeonsi and on zink over NVIDIA's Vulkan driver.
 glClear, fixed-function drawing and gl_FragColor already reached both.
 .
 The state tracker now makes a variant of the fragment shader while one
 glDrawBuffer constant names several buffers of the window's framebuffer: the
 output at location 0 becomes the color the drivers write to every buffer,
 and the other color outputs go, since their draw buffers are GL_NONE in that
 state. A framebuffer object, glDrawBuffers() with one buffer per output, and
 a shader that blends from two sources keep the shader as it is.
 .
 Measured with tests/glx-back-both-eyes.c under Stereo KWin: red before and
 green after on radeonsi and on zink over NVIDIA 615.71.09; the modes that
 already worked (gl_FragColor, glClear, fixed function) stay green; the
 program's capture with stereo off differs by 0 pixels.
 .
 Searched on 2026-11-14 in Mesa's issues and merge requests ("GL_BACK stereo
 fragment output", "DrawBuffer GL_BACK stereo", "FRONT_AND_BACK fragment
 output", "WRITES_ALL_CBUFS stereo", "quad buffer stereo right buffer"):
 nothing on it.
